#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193914 :tutorial/content "Let's consider the following inequality: \n\n$$\n7x -2 < 5x - 18\n$$\n\nAs usual, we solve the inequality just like we would solve an equation.\n\nFirst, we apply the addition principle. We start by subtracting $5x$ from both sides: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7x-2 & <5x-18 \\\\ 7x-2-5x & <5x-18-5x \\\\ 2x-2 & <-18\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we add $2$ to both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-2 & <-18 \\\\ 2x-2+2 & <-18+2 \\\\ 2x & <-16\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we use the multiplication principle, dividing both sides by $2{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x & <-16 \\\\ \\frac{2x}{2} & <\\frac{-16}{2} \\\\ x & <-8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x < -8.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "e1c6e185-0d13-5ea0-86f9-e61f64898f5f" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8106 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193915 :tutorial/content "Now, let's solve an inequality that contains a rational expression:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2y-1}{3} \\lt 5\n$$\n\nFirst, we clear the rational expression by multiplying both sides by $3{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2y-1}{3} & <5 \\\\ 3\\cdot (\\frac{2y-1}{3}) & <3\\cdot 5 \\\\ \\cancel{3}\\cdot (\\frac{2y-1}{\\cancel{3}}) & <15 \\\\ 2y-1 & <15\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the addition and multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2y-1 & <15 \\\\ 2y-1+1 & <15+1 \\\\ 2y & <16 \\\\ \\frac{2y}{2} & <\\frac{16}{2} \\\\ y & <8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $y <  8.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "31078899-8e97-5c79-a990-df51f4a9f773" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8107 :tutorial/title "Clearing Rational Expression in Linear Inequalities"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193916 :tutorial/content "Finally, let's consider an inequality that contains rational expressions on both sides:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x+1}{4} \\leq \\dfrac{x}{2}\n$$\n\nThe rational expressions on both sides of this inequality have positive denominators $4$ and $2.$ The least common multiple of $4$ and $2$ is ${\\color{red}{4}}.$\nThus, we can clear the rational expressions by multiplying both sides of the inequality ${\\color{red}{4}}.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x+1}{4} & \\le \\frac{x}{2} \\\\ 4\\cdot (\\frac{x+1}{4}) & \\le 4\\cdot (\\frac{x}{2}) \\\\ x+1 & \\le 2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the addition and multiplication principles. \n\nRemember, when we divide an inequality by a negative number, we need to flip the inequality sign:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+1 & \\le 2x \\\\ x+1-2x & \\le 2x-2x \\\\ 1-x & \\le 0 \\\\ 1-x-1 & \\le 0-1 \\\\ -x & \\le -1 \\\\ \\frac{-x}{(-1)} & \\,\\,\\ge \\,\\,\\frac{-1}{(-1)} \\\\ x & \\ge 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x\\geq 1.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "5ec24500-fe0f-5068-b9fa-f19ce733798a" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8110 :tutorial/title "Clearing Two Rational Expressions in Linear Inequalities"}]}
